# Line endings are pinned, not left to whoever clones this.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting, so without this file the same commit checks out
# differently on two developers's disks. `prosperous.sh` is the sharp end: a CR on the shebang
# line makes it unrunnable anywhere but Windows, and the error names the interpreter rather
# than the line ending. See [OOPS conventions section 8](https://github.com/project-oops/OOPS/blob/main/docs/CONVENTIONS.md#8-gates).
#
# `text=auto` rather than a list of extensions: git detects binary content itself, and a list
# only protects the extensions somebody remembered. A fixture that must not be touched at all
# needs an explicit `-text` line below - `eol=lf` still rewrites a file that legitimately
# contains CRLF, which a captured line protocol might.
* text=auto eol=lf
